/*
Problem: Convert Sorted Array to Binary Search Tree (LC 108)
Topic: Binary Search Tree / Divide and Conquer

--------------------------------------------------

Idea:

The array is already sorted.

To build a height-balanced BST,
choose the middle element as the root.

Elements on the left become the left
subtree.

Elements on the right become the
right subtree.

Repeat the same process recursively.

--------------------------------------------------

Algorithm:

1. If the current range is empty,
   return NULL.

2. Find the middle index.

3. Create a node using the middle
   element.

4. Recursively build:

   • Left subtree from the left half.

   • Right subtree from the right half.

5. Return the root.

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

nums = [-10,-3,0,5,9]

Middle:

0

Tree:

          0
         / \
      -10   5
        \    \
        -3    9

(Any height-balanced BST is valid.)

--------------------------------------------------

Example:

Input:

nums = [1,3]

Possible BST:

    1
     \
      3

or

    3
   /
  1

Both are balanced.

--------------------------------------------------

Why It Works?

Choosing the middle element divides
the array into two nearly equal
halves.

This keeps the height of the BST
approximately balanced.

--------------------------------------------------

Time : O(n)

Each element is used exactly once.

Space : O(log n)

Recursion stack for a balanced tree.

Worst case: O(n)

--------------------------------------------------

Pattern:

Divide and Conquer / Binary Search Tree
*/

class Solution {
public:

    TreeNode* helper(vector<int>& nums,
                     int st,
                     int end) {

        if (st > end)
            return NULL;

        int mid = st + (end - st) / 2;

        // Create root
        TreeNode* root =
            new TreeNode(nums[mid]);

        // Build left subtree
        root->left =
            helper(nums, st, mid - 1);

        // Build right subtree
        root->right =
            helper(nums, mid + 1, end);

        return root;
    }

    TreeNode* sortedArrayToBST(
        vector<int>& nums) {

        return helper(nums, 0,
                      nums.size() - 1);
    }
};
